Rheumatoid Arthritis
Rheumatology & Immunology
Symmetrical polyarthritis, MCP/PIP joint involvement, Anti-CCP positivity

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: RHI-040
Reason for Visit: Acute, severe exacerbation of widespread, symmetrical hand and wrist swelling, causing debilitating morning stiffness, agonizing pain upon gripping, and an inability to perform basic self-care.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and spouse
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage noted an acute, functionally immobilizing flare of symmetrical polyarthritis with severe systemic fatigue. Urgent after-hours physical assessment was strictly required to rule out a superimposed acute infection, evaluate the degree of functional loss (inability to dress or eat independently), and prescribe immediate bridging systemic corticosteroids to rapidly restore manual dexterity and independence prior to the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My hands are so stiff and swollen I can't even hold my toothbrush, the pain is agonizing, and it takes me hours to get moving every morning."
History of Present Illness: The patient reports an insidious onset, now culminating in an acute flare, of symmetrical, additive polyarthritis predominantly involving the small joints of the hands and wrists. Over the past 7 days, the patient has experienced pronounced morning stiffness lasting longer than three hours, which only marginally improves with activity. The patient describes agonizing pain upon gripping objects, indicative of severe tenosynovitis leading to profoundly reduced grip strength. Complains of systemic malaise, severe exhaustion, and low-grade fevers accompanying the joint swelling. Notes firm bumps on the elbows.
Functional Status: Housebound; completely dependent on their spouse for dressing, feeding, and toileting due to severe impairment of manual dexterity.
Social/Safety Concerns: Severe acute loss of independence; high distress regarding the rapid functional decline.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 130/80 mmHg | HR: 95 bpm | Temp: 37.6 C | RR: 16 bpm | SpO2: 98% | Wt: 70 Kg
General: Patient appears chronically fatigued, holding both hands elevated on a pillow, wincing when the fingers are passively moved.
Physical Exam: Musculoskeletal: Both hands demonstrate classic, symmetrical polyarthritis. There is palpable, spongy, warm synovial hypertrophy (pannus) evident at the metacarpophalangeal (MCP) and proximal interphalangeal (PIP) joints bilaterally. Crucially, there is complete sparing of the distal interphalangeal (DIP) joints. Wrists are swollen, warm, and highly tender, with severely restricted range of motion. Grip strength is 1/5 bilaterally due to pain and mechanical block. Firm, non-tender subcutaneous rheumatoid nodules are palpated on the extensor surfaces of both forearms near the olecranon. No gross ulnar deviation or swan-neck deformities are yet present, suggesting earlier-stage but highly active disease. Diagnostic tetrad is clinically perfect: prominent symmetrical polyarthritis, classic MCP/PIP joint involvement, strongly anticipating strongly positive anti-cyclic citrullinated peptide (anti-CCP) antibodies and positive rheumatoid factor on formal serology.
Environmental Observations: Patient is wearing oversized, loose clothing as they cannot manage buttons or zippers.
Medication Reconciliation: Taking maximum doses of paracetamol and ibuprofen with minimal relief of the profound stiffness.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Rheumatoid Arthritis - Status: Exacerbated (Acute severe systemic flare)
Differentials:

Osteoarthritis Flare - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The classic involvement of MCP and PIP joints while sparing the DIPs (OA classically involves the DIPs), the presence of spongy, warm synovial pannus (inflammatory), and the profound, prolonged morning stiffness (>3 hours).

Viral Polyarthritis (e.g., Parvovirus B19) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The presence of subcutaneous rheumatoid nodules (which strongly indicate established, seropositive RA) and the progressive, additive nature of the flare.

Systemic Lupus Erythematosus (SLE) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The absence of typical cutaneous lupus signs (malar rash), no evidence of severe organ involvement (pleuritis/nephritis), and the prominent, destructive-pattern synovitis which is more classic for RA.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate (Complete loss of manual dexterity)

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with an archetypal, hyper-inflammatory flare of Rheumatoid Arthritis, characterized by severe, symmetrical small-joint synovitis and profound functional immobilization of the hands. The intensity of the morning stiffness and the complete inability to perform basic ADLs necessitated an urgent after-hours bedside assessment. It was critical to physically confirm the inflammatory, boggy nature of the synovitis, rule out septic joints, and most importantly, initiate an immediate rescue course of oral corticosteroids. Deferring this care until standard hours would have left the patient in severe, unmanageable distress and fully dependent on a caregiver, firmly satisfying the MBS 585-600 criteria.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Initiate a bridging course of oral corticosteroids (e.g., Prednisolone 15-20mg daily, stepping down) to rapidly suppress the acute synovial inflammation and restore manual function. Arrange an urgent referral to a Rheumatologist for initiation or escalation of Disease-Modifying Antirheumatic Drugs (DMARDs) to prevent irreversible joint destruction. Request routine GP follow-up for baseline bloods (RF, anti-CCP, ESR, CRP) and baseline hand/wrist X-rays.
Positive Behavioral Change: Medication timing optimization: Take the Prednisolone dose extremely early in the morning (e.g., 6:00 AM) or even split a small dose to the late evening; executing this precise timing directly blunts the peak of the inflammatory cytokines that cause the agonizing morning stiffness, immediately proving the value of the medication. Warm water immersion: Soak hands in a basin of warm water for 10 minutes upon waking; observing that the gentle heat physically relaxes the tense tendons and eases the morning 'gel' phenomenon will reinforce the use of non-pharmacological symptom management.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 3-5 days to assess the clinical response to the steroid bridge and coordinate the Rheumatology referral.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Present to the Emergency Department or regular GP urgently if a single joint suddenly becomes dramatically more swollen, intensely red, and hot compared to the others, or if you develop a high swinging fever with uncontrollable shivering (indicating a superimposed septic joint).

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: The clinical signs are classic for a severe flare of Rheumatoid Arthritis. The prescribed steroids will act as a rapid "fire extinguisher" for the joint swelling, but definitive diagnosis requires specific blood tests.
Clinical Course: With oral steroids, the severe morning stiffness and joint pain typically improve dramatically within 48 to 72 hours. However, you must see a specialist soon to start long-term medications that protect your joints from permanent damage.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

The steroid medication will quickly reduce the swelling, but it also slightly lowers your immune system. If one specific joint suddenly blows up, turns bright red, and is too hot to touch, you must seek emergency medical help to ensure an infection hasn't entered the joint.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Your immune system is mistakenly attacking the delicate lining (synovium) of your joints, causing them to fill with inflammatory fluid and boggy tissue, which makes them stiffen severely overnight. The Prednisolone is a powerful anti-inflammatory that will quickly shut down this attack and allow you to use your hands again.
Actionable Steps: Take the Prednisolone strictly early in the morning with breakfast. Soak your hands in warm water to ease the morning stiffness. Do not attempt forceful gripping until the swelling has visibly reduced.

